WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.349
Book Three, Proposition Nineteen.

2
00:00:02.349 --> 00:00:13.164
If a straight line touch a circle, and from the point of contact a straight line be drawn at right angles to the tangent, the center of the circle will be on the straight line so drawn.

3
00:00:13.164 --> 00:00:19.945
Let the straight line D E touch the circle A B C at the point C, and from C let C A be drawn at right angles to D E.

4
00:00:19.945 --> 00:00:22.810
We claim that the center of the circle is on A C.

5
00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:31.560
For suppose it is not, but, if possible, let a point F outside A C be the center, and let C F be joined.

6
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:46.189
Since the straight line D E touches the circle A B C, and F C has been joined from the supposed center to the point of contact, by Three Eighteen F C is perpendicular to D E; therefore the angle F C E is right.

7
00:00:46.189 --> 00:00:57.826
But the angle A C E was also drawn right; therefore by Postulate Four angle F C E equals angle A C E—the part equal to the whole, which by Common Notion Five is impossible.

8
00:00:57.826 --> 00:01:05.269
Therefore F is not the center of circle A B C; and similarly neither is any other point except a point on A C.

9
00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:08.449
Therefore the center of the circle lies on A C.

10
00:01:08.449 --> 00:01:10.140
As was required to prove.

11
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:19.486
Proposition Three Nineteen completes the triad of circular tangency: the normal to a tangent at the point of contact necessarily passes through the circle's center.

12
00:01:19.486 --> 00:01:32.386
As Heath notes, combining Three Nineteen with Three Eleven and Three Twelve immediately establishes that two circles touching internally or externally share a common tangent perpendicular to their line of centers at the point of contact.

13
00:01:32.386 --> 00:01:44.360
